Here's how to implement this in .NET MAUI Shell:

## 1. Override `OnBackButtonPressed` on your page

```csharp
public partial class EditProductPage : ContentPage
{
    private bool _hasUnsavedChanges;

    protected override bool OnBackButtonPressed()
    {
        if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
        {
            // Fire-and-forget the async confirmation
            Dispatcher.Dispatch(async () =>
            {
                bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
                    "Unsaved Changes",
                    "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
                    "Discard", "Stay");

                if (leave)
                    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
            });

            return true; // cancel the default back navigation
        }

        return false; // allow normal navigation
    }
}
```

Returning `true` suppresses the hardware/software back button. The dispatcher launches the async dialog safely.

## 2. Custom "Cancel" toolbar button

In your XAML, replace the default back arrow with a shell back button override and a custom toolbar item:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="MyApp.Views.EditProductPage"
             Shell.BackButtonBehavior="{StaticResource HiddenBackButton}">

    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior IsVisible="False" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>

    <Shell.TitleView>
        <HorizontalStackLayout Spacing="8" VerticalOptions="Center">
            <Button Text="Cancel"
                    Clicked="OnCancelClicked"
                    BackgroundColor="Transparent"
                    TextColor="{AppThemeBinding Light=Black, Dark=White}" />
            <Label Text="Edit Product" FontSize="18" VerticalOptions="Center" />
        </HorizontalStackLayout>
    </Shell.TitleView>

    <!-- page content -->
</ContentPage>
```

## 3. Handle the Cancel button click

```csharp
private async void OnCancelClicked(object sender, EventArgs e)
{
    if (_hasUnsavedChanges)
    {
        bool leave = await DisplayAlert(
            "Unsaved Changes",
            "You have unsaved changes. Discard and go back?",
            "Discard", "Stay");

        if (!leave) return;
    }

    await Shell.Current.GoToAsync("..");
}
```

## Key points

| Concern | Mechanism |
|---------|-----------|
| Hardware back (Android) | `OnBackButtonPressed()` returning `true` |
| Shell back arrow | `BackButtonBehavior.IsVisible = False` hides it |
| Custom button | Toolbar/TitleView `Button` with confirmation logic |
| Swipe-back (iOS) | Not interceptable via Shell; consider disabling with a platform-specific handler if needed |

For iOS swipe-back, you can disable it in a custom handler:

```csharp
#if IOS
Microsoft.Maui.Handlers.PageHandler.Mapper.AppendToMapping("NoSwipeBack", (handler, view) =>
{
    if (view is EditProductPage)
    {
        var nc = handler.ViewController?.NavigationController;
        if (nc != null)
            nc.InteractivePopGestureRecognizer.Enabled = false;
    }
});
#endif
```

This gives you full control over back navigation with an unsaved-changes guard.
